new file mode 100644
index 000000000..25e331517
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/composables/motion/engage/stage.ts
@@ -0,0 +1,1285 @@
+// THE ENGAGED STAGE: the ladder's ENGAGED rung (`engageLadder.ts`), built once, for every
+// control of the `held` class whose door asks for it (useEngage's `stage`).
+//
+// IN PLACE UNDER EVERY MODALITY (Q-3′ (i), INBOUND O-103f). A fine pointer's hold, a
+// touch's hold and a keyboard engagement all grow the control where it stands: no top
+// layer, no footprint, no clip or mask written, no anchor. It stays a child of every
+// ancestor it has at rest, so it paints what its place paints (every clip, mask, radius,
+// opacity, filter and transform) in the frame its place moves. The grow is the held
+// class's own recipe (utilities/engage.css `engage-held`), capped along the inline axis by
+// the reach this module writes (`--stage-reach`). An engaged control grows inside its own
+// stacking context, so a later positioned sibling can paint over it. No engagement veils
+// the world (O-103d (5)). The held host is never promoted: only its lens enters the top
+// layer, so the host's `:first-child`, `+` and `~` matches do not change.
+//
+// THE INVARIANT IS ON PAINT (P-E14 §S14-a). Read once per frame, the held host's painted
+// place never steps on the frame a re-render lands (≤ 0.5 px on that frame), measured
+// against what carries it. What carries it is every transform between the host and the
+// viewport, whatever writes it, a relatively positioned ancestor's inset, and the scroll
+// the user or a script asks for: a carrier's move is carried, at any rate, and the stage
+// writes nothing for it. Its place is where the engine lays it out: a move of the place is
+// glided from where it was painted, on the `dock` row. A place moving at constant velocity
+// up to 4 px a frame (a layout animation) is carried after its onset; its onset and every
+// departure from that velocity are glided; a faster move is a step whose velocity is not
+// carried (P-E15 §S15-a rule 5).
+// The stage's own glide is a `translate`, which changes no layout value, so nothing
+// re-glides it and it lands.
+//
+// THE FRAME READ (§S14-b, as amended by §S15). While a host is engaged, the stage reads in
+// the frame's resize step (after layout, before paint), reads first and writes after.
+//   K  the layout key, from the engine's layout values only: the host's offsetParent chain;
+//      each level in root px (a link's offsets × its own effective zoom, plus its
+//      offsetParent's client edge × that parent's zoom, not <body>; K3) and their sum Q; the
+//      host's size; each link's `currentCSSZoom` and its offsetParent's; each link's size;
+//      every scroll ancestor's offsets S and ranges R. The scrollers, the sticky links, the
+//      carriers the flat tree holds and the host's alignment point are read at the hold
+//      and when the link set changes.
+//   C  the carriers, from computed values: each link's linear map (rotate · scale · the
+//      linear part of `transform`), whether it is projective, in a 3D rendering context or
+//      on an `offset-path`, its origin, the percentage share of its translation (Typed OM,
+//      percentages kept) and a relatively positioned link's inset. Read on a frame whose
+//      layout key changed, whose painted box changed less the stage's own glides, or while
+//      a glide it answers must follow the carriers' map; never on a still frame.
+//   P  the host's painted reference point, less the painted offset of the stage's own
+//      running glides.
+// A move of a level, at any depth, is mapped to the host's px through the linear part of
+// the carriers above that level, read from their computed values on that frame:
+// t = −J⁻¹ · Σᵢ L₍>ᵢ₎ · Δᵢ, with J the host's zoom times every carrier's linear part
+// (rule 1). A carrier's size change adds its size-resolved share (its origin's move through
+// I − L, and its percentage translation), and a relative inset's change is the carrier's,
+// not its level's. A level is measured on paint instead (rule 2) where a projective or
+// `offset-path` carrier sits above it, where a link at or above it is in a 3D rendering
+// context, where a carrier the chain cannot see sits above it (a shadow tree's, found by the
+// flat-tree walk, or by the hold's detector: the computed map against the engine's own), and
+// where its offsetParent is zoomed or its own zoom changed in the frame. On paint the step is
+// the displacement of the host's reference point, solved on the engine's own paint by paused
+// composite-add probes of the host's `translate`, true Newton (the map re-asked at each
+// iterate, at most 4); a probe adds back any scroll the engine answers it with, and the
+// written glide is read where it lands and corrected on the same map, at most 4 times (M14).
+// The host's own size change, its zoom included, moves only its alignment point (the point
+// its layout keeps: start, centre or end per axis, read from computed values), so a change
+// that keeps that point glides nothing (§S15-h A3). A glide answering a level at or above a
+// transformed link is re-derived on each frame the carriers' map changes, so it paints on
+// the step's own path under the current map (rule 4). A layout animation's running velocity
+// is carried and only its change from frame to frame is glided, past the measure's
+// resolution (0.5 px on paint, 1 layout px on the chain).
+//
+// STATED LIMITS (§S14-b.4, b.6, b.11, §S15), each read in both engines by its cell: a step
+// under one layout px that changes no rounded offset is painted as laid out; a step on a
+// paint route holds one frame of a carrier written every frame (M6's bound), and a
+// paint-route glide is solved at the act's frame; Newton's extremal projective corner, and
+// the singular edge where the host's depth reaches the perspective distance; a user scroll
+// in a step frame or a range-change frame; anchoring caused off the host's chain with the
+// range unchanged; a re-snap whose item grows inside the scroller's existing overflow;
+// another resize observer's write after this one's read; a closed-root carrier that changes
